The most famous giants were griffinflies, cousins of today's dragonflies. *Meganeura*, found in France, and *Meganeuropsis*, found in Kansas in the USA, had wingspans of about 65 to 70 cm. That is longer than a school ruler and a half, or close to a cricket bat.

They had huge eyes and spiny legs for grabbing prey in mid-air. Sharp jaws then chewed up other insects and maybe small amphibians.

{{story:insect-flight}}

Uyuni sits about 3,650 m above sea level, higher than most of the Himalayan hill stations. Its surface is astonishingly level. Across the whole salar, the height changes by less than a metre.

In the rainy season, a few centimetres of water lie on top. With no wind, the water reflects the sky perfectly, and the horizon disappears. Tourists take photos where the clouds seem to be under their feet. Because it is so flat and so bright, scientists have used Uyuni to check the measuring instruments on satellites that bounce lasers off Earth's surface.

{{story:batteries-phones}}

Start with the dining table. Salt is crystals of halite, which is mined from old seabeds or dried from seawater in [[@salt-pans|salt pans]] along the coasts of Gujarat and Tamil Nadu. Steel spoons are mostly iron, smelted from ores like haematite. Odisha and Jharkhand hold some of India's biggest iron ore mines.

In the bathroom, [[@made-toothpaste|toothpaste]] often contains fine powders of chalk or silica, which gently scrub teeth. The fluoride in many toothpastes comes from minerals too. Talcum powder is ground-up talc, the softest mineral there is. White porcelain sinks and cups are made from a clay mineral called kaolinite, fired in a hot [[kiln|kiln]].

{{story:glass-made}}

In 1705, the English astronomer Edmond Halley noticed that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 followed almost the same path. He guessed they were one comet and predicted it would return in 1758. Halley died before then, but the comet appeared right on schedule, and it was named after him.

When [[@comets-famous|Halley's Comet]] came back in 1910, Earth passed through its tail. Some people panicked and bought "comet pills" to protect themselves. Nothing happened, because the tail is spread so thin.

{{story:why-ice-floats}}

For flashing fireflies, light is a language. A male flies through the dusk flashing his species' pattern. A female watching from the grass answers with her own flash after a precise delay. If the timing is right, he flies down to her.

Some fireflies cheat. Females of a group called *Photuris*, found in the Americas, copy the reply of another species. When a hopeful male lands, she eats him. That's why scientists call them "femme fatale" fireflies.

{{story:anglerfish}}

This stage is called pre-production, and it is mostly organising. Who plays which part? Where will each scene be shot? Can a desert scene be filmed near Jaisalmer, or does it need a studio?

Planners break the script into pieces and group them. If five scenes happen in the same school corridor, they're shot together, even if they appear at the start, middle and end. That is why films are almost always made out of order.

{{story:special-effects}}

The wall was more than a barrier. It had thousands of watchtowers, built so guards could see from one to the next. When raiders approached, soldiers lit fires at night or sent up smoke by day, passing warnings along the wall faster than a rider on horseback could.

Gates let traders and travellers through, and soldiers collected taxes there. Garrison towns grew up nearby. In that way, the wall worked a little like a guarded border, the kind you can read about in [[@borders-why|why countries have borders]].

{{story:astronaut-life}}

Many children in Rajasthan's [[@villages-india|villages]] herd goats or help on farms during the day. So the college runs night schools, lit by solar lamps, where lessons fit around work. In 2008, about 3,000 children went to 150 such schools, and girls far outnumbered boys.

The night schools also run a children's parliament. Pupils elect their own members and even a prime minister, who check whether schools have teachers, water and lights, and report problems to adults.

{{story:rainwater-harvesting}}

In ancient India, many students lived with a teacher, or guru, at a gurukul, learning texts by heart, along with grammar, astronomy and other subjects. Later came large centres of learning. [[@nalanda|Nalanda]], in present-day Bihar, flourished from about the 5th century CE and drew students from China, Korea and Central Asia. It had libraries, lecture halls and living quarters for thousands of monks and students.

{{story:nalanda}}

Elsewhere, monasteries in Europe and madrasas in the Islamic world taught reading, religion and science. [[@hist-first-university|The University of al-Qarawiyyin]] in Fez, [[@morocco|Morocco]], founded in 859 by a woman named Fatima al-Fihri, is often called the oldest university still operating.

The trouble is that Earth takes about 365.2422 days to [[orbit|orbit]] the Sun. A 355-day year drifted badly, so priests kept adding extra months, sometimes for political reasons. By the time of Julius Caesar, festivals were landing in the wrong [[@seasons-why|seasons]].

In 46 BCE Caesar brought in a 365-day year, worked out with the help of the astronomer Sosigenes of Alexandria. Every fourth year got one extra day, and it went into February. That gave us the [[@leap-years|leap year]].

{{story:ancient-rome}}

People needed fair weights as much as fair lengths. In the [[@indus-cities|Indus Valley cities]], about 4,500 years ago, traders used carefully cut cubes of stone. Archaeologists have found that their sizes follow a regular pattern, with each weight a set multiple of a smaller one. A shopkeeper in Harappa and one in Lothal could trust each other's scales.

Later, India used units like the *tola*, *seer* and *maund*. The tola, about 11.7 grams, is still used by many jewellers when they sell [[@gold-where|gold]].

{{story:indus-cities}}

Every spring at Cooper's Hill in Gloucestershire, England, someone rolls a round cheese weighing about 3 to 4 kg off the top of a hill. A crowd of runners chases it. The hill is so steep, in places nearly as steep as a staircase, that most competitors fall and roll most of the way down. The first to cross the finish line wins the cheese.

The cheese gets a head start and is almost never caught. In some years a foam copy has been rolled instead, for safety, with the real cheese handed to the winner at the bottom. Ambulances wait at the foot of the hill every year. Nobody knows exactly when the race began, but it has been run for at least 200 years.

{{story:harry-potter-effect}}

Going up too fast can make people ill. Acute mountain sickness can bring a headache, sickness, tiredness and trouble sleeping. It often starts above about 2,500 metres. Rarely, fluid can build up in the lungs or brain, which is a medical emergency.

The best treatment is simple: go back down. Climbers follow a rule of "climb high, sleep low", and try not to raise their sleeping height by more than about 300–500 metres a day once they are above 3,000 metres. Anyone feeling unwell in the mountains should tell an adult straight away.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

Your [[@skin|skin]] is always replacing itself. New cells form at the bottom, move up over about a month, then die and drop off. You shed tens of thousands of cells every minute. Some of the dust in your home is old skin.

When the scalp is irritated, it speeds this up a lot. Cells reach the surface before they have dried out and separated properly. Instead of falling as fine powder, they stick together with oil into visible flakes.

{{story:gross-skin-shedding}}

Before 1922, children with type 1 diabetes usually lived only a short time after being diagnosed. Their pancreas had stopped making insulin, the hormone that lets cells take in sugar from the blood.

In 1921, at the University of Toronto in Canada, Frederick Banting and his student assistant Charles Best managed to extract insulin from animal pancreases. In January 1922, a 14-year-old boy named Leonard Thompson became the first person to receive an insulin injection for diabetes. After an improved version, he recovered dramatically. Banting and his boss John Macleod won the Nobel Prize in 1923. Today millions of people with diabetes live full lives with insulin.

{{story:sugar}}

A [[@solar-panels|solar panel]] turns light straight into electricity, with no moving parts. Twenty years ago, solar power was among the most expensive ways to make electricity. Since then, the price of solar panels has fallen by around 90%. Now, in sunny places, it is often the cheapest electricity there is.

India is racing ahead. In July 2026, the country had about 165 gigawatts of solar capacity, enough on a bright noon to run about 200 crore ceiling fans at once. Huge solar parks now cover patches of desert in Rajasthan and Gujarat. The government aims to reach 500 gigawatts of capacity from non-fossil sources by 2030.

{{story:solar-india}}

English letters sit in a row, one after another. Most [[@scripts-india|Indian scripts]] work differently. Each consonant carries a built-in "a" sound, and you change the vowel by adding a small sign, called a matra, above, below, before or after it. In Hindi, कि (ki) puts its vowel sign before the letter, even though you hear it after.

Some consonants also join into one shape. क and ष together make क्ष, which looks nothing like either. A keyboard can't have a key for every combination. So the computer has to work out the joining after you type, using rules built into the font and the system.

{{story:languages-india}}

Sailing ships depended on the wind. Around the Bay of Bengal, the monsoon makes that wind dependable. From about June to September it blows from the southwest, pushing ships from India towards Southeast Asia. From about November it turns and blows the other way.

So voyages followed a calendar. Sailors left with one monsoon, traded or fought, and waited for the wind to turn before sailing home. We don't have drawings of Chola warships, so historians argue about how big they were. They were probably wooden ships with sewn or pegged planks and large sails, able to carry soldiers, horses and supplies.

{{story:monsoon}}

Gandhi returned from [[@south-africa|South Africa]] in 1915. In 1919, British troops fired on an unarmed crowd at [[@jallianwala|Jallianwala Bagh]] in Amritsar, killing hundreds of people. The massacre shocked India. Rabindranath Tagore gave up the knighthood the British had given him in protest, and many Indians who had trusted British fairness now turned against British rule.

Gandhi then led the Non-Cooperation Movement of 1920–22, asking Indians to leave British schools, courts and jobs, and to spin their own [[@khadi|khadi]] cloth. On 26 January 1930, the Congress declared that its goal was *purna swaraj*, complete independence. That same year, Gandhi's [[@salt-march|Salt March]] drew the eyes of the world.

{{story:constitution}}

The first Scinde Dawks were pressed into red sealing wax on paper. Wax was a terrible idea in Sindh's heat. It cracked, crumbled and flaked off letters. Very few of these red wax stamps survive, and those that do are among the rarest stamps in the world.

Later versions were pressed into white and blue paper instead. They didn't carry any picture of a king or queen, only the Company's mark, a heart shape divided into sections with the letters "EIC". In 1854 the Scinde Dawk was withdrawn when stamps for all of India took over.

{{story:oddity-hindustani-mail}}

Dorothy Vaughan was a maths teacher before she joined Langley in 1943. In 1949 she became head of the West Area unit, the first Black supervisor at the agency.

She saw the future coming. When IBM's electronic computers arrived, she taught herself and her team the programming language FORTRAN. When the segregated unit was closed in 1958, many of her staff moved straight into the new computing work.

{{story:women-maths}}

Older tall buildings needed very thick walls at the bottom to carry the weight above. The higher they went, the thicker the walls had to be. William Le Baron Jenney's Home Insurance Building changed that. A metal frame, like a skeleton, carried the weight. The walls just hung on it like skin.

That meant thinner walls and bigger windows. Add safe [[@elevators|elevators]], and buildings could rise 10, 20, then 100 floors. Architects such as Louis Sullivan designed tall buildings that looked light and modern. This group became known as the Chicago School.

{{story:skyscrapers}}

On many common world maps, Greenland looks as big as Africa. This is because of a map style called the Mercator projection. It was designed in 1569 to help sailors steer, and it stretches places more and more the closer they are to the poles.

In reality, Africa is about 14 times bigger than Greenland. India is bigger than Greenland too. You can find out why flattening a round Earth always distorts something in [[@maps-flat|flat maps]].

{{story:maps-flat}}

Ha Long Bay in the north holds around 1,600 limestone islands and islets that rise straight out of green water. A local legend says a dragon came down from the mountains, and its thrashing tail carved the valleys that the sea later filled.

Further south, in Phong Nha-Ke Bang National Park, a local man named Ho Khanh found a cave entrance in the jungle around 1990. In 2009, British and Vietnamese cavers explored it and found Son Doong, the largest known cave passage on Earth. Some chambers could hold a 40-storey building. Parts of the roof have collapsed, letting in sunlight, so a jungle grows on the cave floor. The cave even has its own clouds. It was carved by an underground river dissolving limestone over millions of years, the way [[@caves-form|caves form]] in rock all over the world.

{{story:caves-form}}

In 1913, [[@tagore|Rabindranath Tagore]] became the first writer from outside Europe to win, mainly for his book of poems Gitanjali. His prize made news around the world and brought Indian literature to many new readers.

[[@kipling-india|Rudyard Kipling]], author of [[@jungle-book|The Jungle Book]], was born in Bombay in 1865 and spent his early childhood there. He won in 1907, at 41, and is still the youngest literature winner. V. S. Naipaul, a writer of Indian descent born in Trinidad, won in 2001.

{{story:nobel-peace}}

In the 10th century, a writer named Ibn Sayyar al-Warraq collected recipes from the courts of Baghdad. Among them was a fried batter sweet drenched in syrup. Another cookbook from Baghdad, written in the 1200s, describes something similar.

These were sweets for festivals and rich tables. In Iran, a version called zoolbia is still eaten during Ramadan. In Tunisia and Algeria, a sweet called zlabia fills shop windows in the same season. The family resemblance is easy to spot.

{{story:silk-road}}
